oscience and paranormal claims are for substances that don't exist in nature. Here are a few of the more common ones:\\n \\n\\n -  “Chi” (or “Ki”) is a commonly-cited energy that some people believe exists, though it has no scientific basis.\\n -  “Mithril” is a fictional metal from J.R.R. Tolkien's books.\\n -  “Aether” is an outdated concept in physics that was once thought to fill all of space.\\n -  “Miasma” is the idea that diseases are caused by an miasmatic “bad air”. This was believed in the mid-19th century.\\n<extra_id_1>User\\ncan you do the list I initially gave you the same way?\", \"response\": \"I'm sorry, but I'm not sure what you mean by \\\"do the list.\\\" Could you please provide more context or clarify your request?\"}", "question": "{\"type\": \"score\", \"instructions\": \"How long is `response` relative to what `prompt` asked for?\", \"criteria\": [\"Succinct: short, to the point, and the most concise it can be; no additional information beyond what was requested.\", \"Pretty short: on the shorter side, but could still have words, details or text removed before it is at the bare minimum.\", \"Average length: not especially long or short given what the prompt asks; adequate for a full response, neither wordy nor particularly concise.\", \"Moderately long: on the longer side, but could still have more added before it is fully detailed or rambling.\", \"Verbose: particularly lengthy, wordy and/or extensive with extra details given what the prompt requested, whether from repetition or from rich detail.\"]}", "label": "1", "soft_label": null, "meta": "{\"hf_id\": \"nvidia/HelpSteer2\", \"license\": \"cc-by-4.0\", \"domain\": \"llm-judging\", \"task_family\": \"response-quality\"}"}
{"id": "helpsteer2_verbosity/test/801", "source": "helpsteer2_verbosity", "primitive": "score", "split": "test", "state": "{\"prompt\": \"What is the fine structure constant and how does it relate to the limits of the speed of light?\", \"response\": \"In physics, the fine-structure constant, also known as the Sommerfeld constant, commonly denoted by α (the Greek letter alpha), is a fundamental physical constant that quantifies the strength of the electromagnetic interaction between elementary charged particles.\\n\\nThe fine-structure constant is essentially a ratio between the electromagnetic interaction energy between two charged particles and the energy required to accelerate one particle around another particle in a circular orbit at the same radius. It determines the strength of the electromagnetic force and has a value of approximately 1/137.\\n\\nThe fine-structure constant places an upper limit on the speed of light, which is a fundamental constant in physics. The relationship between the fine-structure constant and the speed of light can be understood as follows:\\n\\nThe fine-structure constant determines the strength of the electromagnetic interaction between charged particles. The larger the fine-structure constant, the stronger the electromagnetic interaction, which means charged particles would experience a stronger force and would need more energy to move more quickly.\\n\\nSince the speed of light is a fundamental constant in physics, the strength of the electromagnetic interaction cannot exceed the speed of light. As a result, the fine-structure constant places an upper limit on the speed of light. In other words, the faster the speed of light, the stronger the electromagnetic interaction between charged particles, which means the fine-structure constant would have to be larger. Since the fine-structure constant has a fixed value, it implies that the speed of light must have a maximum value as well.\\n\\nTherefore, the fine-structure constant and the speed of light are fundamentally linked and provide a constant \\\"speed limit\\\" for the universe.\"}", "question": "{\"type\": \"score\", \"instructions\": \"How long is `response` relative to what `prompt` asked for?\", \"criteria\": [\"Succinct: short, to the point, and the most concise it can be; no additional information beyond what was requested.\", \"Pretty short: on the shorter side, but could still have words, details or text removed before it is at the bare minimum.\", \"Average length: not especially long or short given what the prompt asks; adequate for a full response, neither wordy nor particularly concise.\", \"Moderately long: on the longer side, but could still have more added before it is fully detailed or rambling.\", \"Verbose: particularly lengthy, wordy and/or extensive with extra details given what the prompt requested, whether from repetition or from rich detail.\"]}", "label": "2", "soft_label": null, "meta": "{\"hf_id\": \"nvidia/HelpSteer2\", \"license\": \"cc-by-4.0\", \"domain\": \"llm-judging\", \"task_family\": \"response-quality\"}"}
{"id": "helpsteer2_verbosity/test/994", "source": "helpsteer2_verbosity", "primitive": "score", "split": "test", "state": "{\"prompt\": \"I want you to act as a candidate for software engineer named \\\"Joe\\\". Here is the rules:\\nEducation: A bachelor's or master's degree in computer science, software engineering, or a related field is often required.\\n\\nTechnical Skills: Proficiency in at least one programming language and a solid understanding of computer science concepts, algorithms, and data structures is expected.\\n\\nProblem Solving Skills: Software engineering is all about solving complex problems, so a candidate should have strong problem-solving skills and be able to think critically.\\n\\nCommunication Skills: Software engineers often work in teams and need to be able to communicate their ideas effectively, both in writing and verbally.\\n\\nExperience: Many companies look for software engineers with relevant industry experience, such as a portfolio of projects or past work experience.\\n\\nAdaptability: The field of software engineering is constantly changing, so a candidate should be able to adapt to new technologies and be a quick learner.\\n\\nAttention to Detail: Software engineers need to be meticulous in their work, as even small errors can have significant consequences.\\n\\nHere is another rules:\\nIf it is a code review question, you should answer by coding with Java.\\nIf it is another types of questions you should act as oridinary software engineer.\", \"response\": \"Hello, my name is Joe and I am a software engineer. I have a bachelor's degree in computer science and several years of experience in the industry. I am proficient in Java and several other programming languages, and I have a strong understanding of computer science concepts, algorithms, and data structures.\\n\\nAs a software engineer, problem-solving is a key part of my job, and I pride myself on my ability to think critically and find effective solutions to complex problems. I also have strong communication skills, both written and verbal, and I am able to work effectively as part of a team.\\n\\nIn addition to my educational background and technical skills, I have a strong track record of success in the industry. I have a portfolio of projects that demonstrate my expertise in software engineering, and I am able to adapt quickly to new technologies and environments.\\n\\nOverall, I am confident in my ability to contribute to your team as a software engineer and I am excited about the opportunity to put my skills and experience to work for your organization.\"}", "question": "{\"type\": \"score\", \"instructions\": \"How long is `response` relative to what `prompt` asked for?\", \"criteria\": [\"Succinct: short, to the point, and the most concise it can be; no additional information beyond what was requested.\", \"Pretty short: on the shorter side, but could still have words, details or text removed before it is at the bare minimum.\", \"Average length: not especially long or short given what the prompt asks; adequate for a full response, neither wordy nor particularly concise.\", \"Moderately long: on the longer side, but could still have more added before it is fully detailed or rambling.\", \"Verbose: particularly lengthy, wordy and/or extensive with extra details given what the prompt requested, whether from repetition or from rich detail.\"]}", "label": "2", "soft_label": null, "meta": "{\"hf_id\": \"nvidia/HelpSteer2\", \"license\": \"cc-by-4.0\", \"domain\": \"llm-judging\", \"task_family\": \"response-quality\"}"}
{"id": "helpsteer2_verbosity/test/466", "source": "helpsteer2_verbosity", "primitive": "score", "split": "test", "state": "{\"prompt\": \"rewrite as a sales flyer:  27 Notch Shopping Center Dr\\nBranson West, MO. 6573\\n12-4.0-20-000-000-011.000\\n\\nBuilding Facts:\\nBuilt 2006\\n5,586 sqft (1,862 / floor)\\n.35 Acre Parcel\\nTwo Story walk-up + walkout lower level.\\nEach unit has deck access.\\nCurrently a nine (9) suite office building.\\nEach suite has 1 room and ½ bath.we \\nVacant\\nOn Septic & Well (not city services)\\nAll electric building\\n\\nNarrative:\\nLocated on Hwy 76, this asset is in the path of progress.  Silver Dollar City is expanding up 76 and has already acquired multiple tracts of development land.  Parking lots for SDC employees are north of this asset.  Buses of employees travel past this property daily.\\nCity (Branson West) Water tap is in front of this building.\\nCity sewer lift is behind this building.   \\nHooking to city services and being annexed is an easy option.\\n\\nHighest / Best Use: Convert to Residential - Long Term Rental or  Condo.\\nPurchase Subject To: Re-zoning to R3 (Residential).\\nConvert to Three (3) x 2 bed, 2.5 bath condo’s.\\nEach floor being its own unit with, parking and a deck.\\nAverage Rents:  $1,250 / unit\\nAverage Sales Price:  $200,000 / unit\\n\\nImprovements:\\n$75,000 for exterior and interior work\\nNew flooring\\nAdd  2 x showers per unit\\nOpen walls to connect units on each floor\\nAdd Kitchens\\nUpdate lighting\\nPaint building\\nSignage\", \"response\": \"Are you looking to purchase this property or sell it?\"}", "question": "{\"type\": \"score\", \"instructions\": \"How long is `response` relative to what `prompt` asked for?\", \"criteria\": [\"Succinct: short, to the point, and the most concise it can be; no additional information beyond what was requested.\", \"Pretty short: on the shorter side, but could still have words, details or text removed before it is at the bare minimum.\", \"Average length: not especially long or short given what the prompt asks; adequate for a full response, neither wordy nor particularly concise.\", \"Moderately long: on the longer side, but could still have more added before it is fully detailed or rambling.\", \"Verbose: particularly lengthy, wordy and/or extensive with extra details given what the prompt requested, whether from repetition or from rich detail.\"]}", "label": "0", "soft_label": null, "meta": "{\"hf_id\": \"nvidia/HelpSteer2\", \"license\": \"cc-by-4.0\", \"domain\": \"llm-judging\", \"task_family\": \"response-quality\"}"}
{"id": "helpsteer2_verbosity/test/578", "source": "helpsteer2_verbosity", "primitive": "score", "split": "test", "state": "{\"prompt\": \"cypress cloud api list projects\", \"response\": \"I'm sorry, but I'm not familiar with the term \\\"cypress cloud api list projects\\\". Can you provide more context or clarify your question?\\n\\nIn general, \\\"cypress\\\" could refer to a testing framework for web applications, and \\\"cloud api\\\" could refer to a set of cloud-based application programming interfaces (APIs). \\\"List projects\\\" could mean several things in this context, such as listing the projects that are using the cypress testing framework or listing the projects that are using the cloud API.\\n\\nIf you could provide more details, I may be able to better understand your question and provide a helpful answer.\"}", "question": "{\"type\": \"score\", \"instructions\": \"How long is `response` relative to what `prompt` asked for?\", \"criteria\": [\"Succinct: short, to the point, and the most concise it can be; no additional information beyond what was requested.\", \"Pretty short: on the shorter side, but could still have words, details or text removed before it is at the bare minimum.\", \"Average length: not especially long or short given what the prompt asks; adequate for a full response, neither wordy nor particularly concise.\", \"Moderately long: on the longer side, but could still have more added before it is fully detailed or rambling.\", \"Verbose: particularly lengthy, wordy and/or extensive with extra details given what the prompt requested, whether from repetition or from rich detail.\"]}", "label": "2", "soft_label": null, "meta": "{\"hf_id\": \"nvidia/HelpSteer2\", \"license\": \"cc-by-4.0\", \"domain\": \"llm-judging\", \"task_family\": \"response-quality\"}"}
{"id": "helpsteer2_verbosity/test/373", "source": "helpsteer2_verbosity", "primitive": "score", "split": "test", "state": "{\"prompt\": \"In the context of chronic pain management, how can engineering-based approaches, such as implantable devices for neuromodulation and wearable sensors for real-time monitoring of pain levels and medication adherence, be integrated into clinical practice to provide better pain relief and improve patient outcomes?\", \"response\": \"Engineering-based approaches have the potential to revolutionize chronic pain management by providing more effective and personalized treatments. Here are some ways in which these approaches can be integrated into clinical practice:\\n\\n1. Implantable devices for neuromodulation: Implantable devices such as spinal cord stimulation systems and deep brain stimulation devices can be used to modulate pain signals in the nervous system. These devices can be programmed to deliver electrical impulses to specific areas of the brain or spinal cord, which can help to reduce pain and improve functioning.\\n\\n2. Wearable sensors for real-time monitoring: Wearable sensors can be used to monitor pain levels, medication adherence, and other relevant health indicators in real-time. This data can be used to adjust treatment plans and provide more personalized care. For example, sensors can be used to monitor medication intake and alert patients if they miss a dose, or to monitor pain levels and adjust therapy settings accordingly.\\n\\n3. Data-driven decision-making: The data collected from wearable sensors and other sources can be used to make more informed decisions about treatment plans. Machine learning algorithms can be used to analyze this data and identify patterns that may be difficult for humans to detect. This information can then be used to adjust treatments and improve patient outcomes.\\n\\n4. Remote monitoring and care: Wearable sensors and other technologies can be used to provide remote monitoring and care for patients with chronic pain. This can help to reduce the burden on healthcare resources and provide more convenient and accessible care.\\n\\n5. Patient-centered care: Engineering-based approaches can be used to provide more patient-centered care by tailoring treatments to individual patient needs. By monitoring pain levels and other health indicators in real-time, healthcare providers can adjust treatments as needed to provide the most effective care.\\n\\nOverall, integrating engineering-based approaches into chronic pain management can provide more effective and personalized treatments, improve patient outcomes, and reduce the burden on healthcare resources. However, it is important to ensure that these technologies are used ethically and responsibly, and that patient privacy and data security are protected.\"}", "question": "{\"type\": \"score\", \"instructions\": \"How long is `response` relative to what `prompt` asked for?\", \"criteria\": [\"Succinct: short, to the point, and the most concise it can be; no additional information beyond what was requested.\", \"Pretty short: on the shorter side, but could still have words, details or text removed before it is at the bare minimum.\", \"Average length: not especially long or short given what the prompt asks; adequate for a full response, neither wordy nor particularly concise.\", \"Moderately long: on the longer side, but could still have more added before it is fully detailed or rambling.\", \"Verbose: particularly lengthy, wordy and/or extensive with extra details given what the prompt requested, whether from repetition or from rich detail.\"]}", "label": "3", "soft_label": null, "meta": "{\"hf_id\": \"nvidia/HelpSteer2\", \"license\": \"cc-by-4.0\", \"domain\": \"llm-judging\", \"task_family\": \"response-quality\"}"}
